Avaiable under Creative Commons Share Alike (CC SA) Licence 

USAGE:    
    python fontconv.py [-e encoding] -f fontname input_file output_file
    default encoding is utf8
INTRODUCTION

In India, a large number of data sources like vernacular newspapers, magazine,
loksabha website use proprietory fonts for displaying content in Indian
languages. The proprietory fonts overload normal unicode values with language
specific characters. 

While proprietory fonts enable content providers to display data in Indian
languages, they make document processing like extracting entities, searching
with in the document very difficult.

Unicode provides codepoints for all scripts and is accepted widely. Therefore,
documents in unicode can be easily processed, indexed and searched.


indic2unicode converts data in proprietory fonts to unicode. It currently
supports Aryan2, Divya and Surekh fonts for devanagari script that is used by
the LokSabha website for publishing debates. It also supports Chanakya, the
ascii font of the Kruti Dev/DevLys family that the Gazette of India is
published in, and Arial Unicode MS as well as Nirmala UI as they come out of
the Gazette pdfs that are set in them. Beyond devanagari it supports three
kannada fonts that the Karnataka gazette is published in, Tunga, which is a
unicode font, and Nudi and Aklite, which are 8-bit ones, as they come out of
those pdfs, and the kannada of Arial Unicode MS and of Nirmala UI as it comes
out of the Karnataka gazettes that are set in them.

MAIN IDEA

The main idea is to tokenize the input text in a given font and then use the
unicode values of those tokens to get the unicode equivalent. 

Unicode values of tokens is specified in langs/* and the regular expression to
identify the tokens is specified in fonts/*.

For tokenization purpose, I use a pure python implementation of lex-yacc namely
<a href="http://www.dabeaz.com/ply/ply.html">ply</a>. I only use the lexing
facility provided by the module.

RE-ORDERING TOKENS

Tokens obtained after tokenization may not be in the right order as there are
language specific rules. For example, in Aryan font Matra_I will appear before
KA because of visual correctness. However, unicode requires that KA should be
followed by MATRA_I rather than preceded by it.

So the tokens obtained after tokenization needs to be reordered.
fonts/basefont.py implements three ways of reordering tokens in the following
order:

1. Compose tokens: This is used for composing multiple tokens into one that
cannot be unambiguously composed during tokenization phase. For example,  In
font specific file, create a dictionary ans assign it to self.composeTokens.In
fonts/hindi/aryan2,py it is specified as  self.composeTokens = {('A', 'MATRA_AA') :
'AA'}. The key is a tuple of n-tokens and the output is one token.

2. Move a token before: A token can be moved backwards in the series of tokens.
For example, fonts/hindi/aryan2.py specifies self.jumpbefore = {'ADHA_RA': 1}
indicating the ADHA_RA should jump one place before.

3. Move a token ahead: A token can be moved ahead in the series of tokens. For
example, fonts/hindi/aryan2.py specifies self.waitdict = {'MATRA_I': 1, 'MATRAIBINDU'
: 1, 'MATRAIBINDU2': 1, 'MATRA_I2': 2} indicating tokens MATRA_I, MATRAIBINDU
and MATRAIBINDU2 should jump 1 step head and token MATRA_I2 should jump two
positions ahead.

TOKENS THAT ARE NOT A STEP

In a font like Chanakya the distance a token has to travel is not fixed
because a syllable is made of a variable number of tokens. Three sets tell
the two reordering passes which tokens are not to be counted as a step:

1. self.jumpover: tokens that a backward jumping token is not counted
against. fonts/hindi/chanakya.py puts all the matras and the signs in it because
the reph is typed after the whole syllable it sits on, so it has to travel
past 'ी' in वर्ती as well as past 'ा' in अर्थात्.

2. self.waitover: tokens that a forward jumping token is not counted against
and that it stays behind. fonts/hindi/chanakya.py puts NUKTA, RAKAR and YAKAR in
it since they belong to the syllable that matra_i has already passed, as in
चिड़िया and प्रिय.

3. self.halftokens: tokens that a forward jumping token is not counted
against but that it is emitted before, since they are the head of the
syllable that is still coming. fonts/hindi/chanakya.py puts all the half
consonants in it, which is what makes पश्चिम come out of if"pe while दिल्ली
comes out of fnYyh.

A UNICODE FONT THAT NEEDS THE SAME TREATMENT

Arial Unicode MS is a unicode font, but the Gazette pdfs that are set in it
carry a broken ToUnicode map: the map holds all the right strings, it just
hands them to the wrong glyphs. The text that comes out of such a pdf is
therefore devanagari that is in the visual order of the glyphs and that is
spelled with the wrong characters for a part of the alphabet, e.g. निर्माण
comes out as जिमावण, where 'ज' is matra_i, 'ि' is na and 'व' is the reph.

fonts/hindi/arialuni.py treats every glyph of that font as a token in exactly the
way the ascii fonts are treated, and the two reordering passes above put
matra_i and the reph back where unicode wants them.

The map cannot be inverted completely. The font has one glyph of matra_i per
width of the syllable that follows it and one glyph of the reph per width of
the syllable it sits on, and the broken map hands those strings to more than
one consonant each: ja, na, pha, sha, ksha and the conjunct bra all come out
as 'ि', and va as well as tha come out as 'र्'. Neither group can be told
apart any more, so 'ि' is read as na and 'र्' is read as va, the most common
member of each. In the same way a nukta is lost, ड़ comes out as plain ड.

Where the syllable that follows tells the two apart, composeTokens is used to
pick the right one. The half sa is handed the string of the half sta, so both
of them come out as 'स्ट्': the one that a full consonant follows is the half
sa of स्टेशनों, while the one that ra follows is the half sta of रजिस्ट्री.

The pdf extraction also puts a space in the middle of a word every now and
then, e.g. रेलवे comes out as 'रे लवे'. That is not a glyph of the font and a
converter cannot tell such a space from a real one, so it is left alone.

REPAIRING THE PDF INSTEAD

None of that guessing is needed if the pdf itself is at hand. The glyphs of
the page are drawn from their glyph ids and the ToUnicode map is not looked
at while drawing, so the page is right and only its extraction is wrong. The
map was built by pairing the glyphs of a run with the characters of that run
one by one, and devanagari shaping moves matra_i to the left of its cluster
and the reph to the right of its syllable, so the pairing slips exactly on
the glyphs that were moved.

tools/fix_tounicode.py builds the map again out of the font program that the
pdf carries, which says what its glyphs are three times over: the cmap of the
subset maps a character to the glyph that draws it, the subset keeps the
uniXXXX name of the glyphs it did not have to rename, and the GSUB of the
font says which glyphs the shaper made out of which other ones, so a conjunct
is spelled out of the glyphs it was made of, a half form comes out as its
consonant and a halant and a below base form as a halant and its consonant.
A glyph that a subset carries neither a name nor a cmap entry nor a rule for
is filled in from another subset of the same font in the same document, which
is where the glyph id of a font one subset drops and another keeps - the
halant of "Nirmala UI,Bold" among them - is found, and what is left after
that is repaired from a table. It only touches the fonts that are known to
carry a broken map. After that the text can be extracted in the usual way:

    doc = pymupdf.open(pdffile)
    ToUnicodeFixer().fix_document(doc)
    text = doc[pagenum].get_text()

The text that comes out is still in the visual order of the glyphs, so it
goes through fonts/glyphs/arialuni_glyphs.py, which does nothing but the two
reordering passes. Unlike the arialuni font above, that path loses nothing:
ja, na, pha, sha and ksha are all still there, and so are va, tha and the
nuktas.

A bar in the middle of a devanagari word, e.g. द|शरथी, is not a glyph that
was mapped wrong. The gazette itself types a bar where a matra_aa belongs
and the page shows a bar, so it is left as it is.

ANOTHER UNICODE FONT WITH THE SAME BROKEN MAP

Nirmala UI is the other unicode font the Gazette is set in, and its pdfs
carry a map that was broken in the very same way. Which glyph ends up with
which string differs from document to document, because it depends on the
first cluster of the document that each glyph was drawn in, so the font needs
a converter of its own: fonts/hindi/nirmalaui.py. There, दिल्ली comes out as कदल्ली
and के comes out as िे, i.e. 'क' is matra_i and 'ि' is ka.

The losses are of the same kind as the ones above. ka and sha, and the
conjunct shcha as well, all come out as 'ि', which is read as ka. The reph is
drawn together with the matra of its syllable, so it comes out as 'ा', 'ी',
'ो' or as nothing at all, and only the reph over a matra_aa can be found
again, by the 'ाा' that कार्यालय leaves behind as िायाा लय. matra_o is handed
no string at all and comes out as the space of its own advance, so को is left
as 'ि' and only the matra_o of a plural ending can be brought back, from the
'ों' that the anusvara behind it carries.

composeTokens does the same work here as it does for Arial Unicode MS. The
three matra_i glyphs of the font carry the strings of ka, of ra and of the
conjunct kta, so a ra in front of ra is a matra (रिज comes out as ररज) and so
is a kta in front of kta (व्यक्ति comes out as व्यक्तक्त). The sta ligature
carries the string of the half sa, so a 'स्' that a matra follows is that
ligature and its ta is put back, which is what turns हस्ाों तररत back into
हस्तांतरित, while a 'स्' that a consonant follows is the half sa of स्थान.

Here too none of that guessing is needed when the pdf itself is at hand.
tools/fix_tounicode.py repairs a Nirmala UI pdf the same way it repairs an
Arial Unicode MS one, and the text of it goes through
fonts/glyphs/nirmalaui_glyphs.py, which is the same two reordering passes and loses
nothing: sha and matra_o and the reph are all still there, so प्रकाशित and को
and सर्वोच्च come out whole rather than as प्रकाकित and क and सवोच्च.

Nirmala UI spells out as two characters what Arial Unicode MS carries as one
glyph - a half form comes out as its consonant and a halant, a rakar as a
halant and a ra - but the lexer reads a half form out of that pair anyway, so
matra_i still waits for the whole cluster and 'ि' + 'क' + '्र' comes out as
क्रि. The reph is drawn together with the matra of the syllable it sits on,
which is one glyph of the font and two characters of the repaired map, and
the matra is one of the tokens the reph jumps over on its way back, so
निकटवर्ती is drawn as िनकटवतीर् and comes out whole all the same.

Nirmala UI sets the Karnataka gazette as well, and the kannada of a repaired
one goes through fonts/kannada/nirmalaui.py - the same split on the script
that Arial Unicode MS does, the font being one font. The subset of it that
those pdfs carry keeps neither a cmap nor a post nor a GSUB, so there is
nothing in the font to read a glyph out of and its map is short rather than
wrong: it names the letters of the block and stops, and every glyph the
shaper made extracts as (cid:3197) and the like. Those glyphs are put back
from NIRMALA_UI_KANNADA in tools/fix_tounicode.py, which is a table of the
blocks the font lays them out in - the vattus from 3161, then fifteen glyphs
per consonant from 3197, one for the consonant with each of the twelve vowel
signs and one each for it with the anusvara, the visarga and its virama.

What is left after that is the order, and there Nirmala UI draws kannada the
way Arial Unicode MS draws it, so fonts/kannada/nirmalaui.py is that pass
whole: ಕರ್ನಾಟಕ comes out of the repaired pdf as ಕನಾರ್ಟಕ and ಅನುಸೂಚಿಯಲ್ಲಿ as
ಅನುಸೂಚಿಯಲಿ್ಲ, exactly as they do there. The two fonts draw a syllable in a
different number of glyphs - a whole matra is drawn into the letter here, ಕೇ
being one glyph where Arial Unicode MS draws ಕಿ and a length mark, and ಕ್ಷ is
spelled out rather than drawn whole - but that is a difference in what the
repair writes and not in the order it writes it in.



A KANNADA FONT WHOSE MAP IS SOUND AND WHOSE ORDER IS NOT

Tunga is the unicode kannada font that the Karnataka gazette is published
in, and the pdfs that are set in it carry a ToUnicode map that is nearly
sound: every glyph carries the characters it stands for. What is wrong with
the text is the order, because the glyphs are stored in the order in which
they are drawn and kannada draws a syllable as

    base [first half of the matra] vattu(s) [length mark] [anusvara]

while unicode wants the base, the vattus and then the whole matra. So a
vattu comes out behind the matra of its syllable and spelled the other way
round, the consonant before the virama rather than after it, and ಪ್ರಸ್ತಾವನೆ
comes out as ಪರ್ಸಾತ್ವನೆ and ಸಲ್ಲಿಸಿದ as ಸಲಿಲ್ಸಿದ. The arkavattu is drawn on top of
the syllable it belongs to and is stored behind the whole of it, so ಕರ್ನಾಟಕ
comes out as ಕನಾರ್ಟಕ and ಸರ್ಕಾರದ as ಸಕಾರ್ರದ, which is the same journey the reph
of devanagari makes.

A two part matra comes out in its two parts - ೀ as ಿ + ೕ, ೇ as ೆ + ೕ, ೊ as
ೆ + ೂ - and a vattu of that syllable stands between them, so ಶ್ರೀ comes out
as ಶಿ + ರ್ + ೕ. Unicode has one character for each of these matras and NFC
will not compose them, being composition exclusions, so fonts/kannada/tunga.py
puts the halves back together itself.

fonts/kannada/tunga.py reads every syllable of the text as a whole and writes
it out again in the order unicode wants, rather than moving single tokens the
way the devanagari fonts do: the distance a vattu would have to travel is
not a number of tokens, it is however many matra glyphs its syllable has.

WHAT THE TEXT OF A TUNGA PDF CANNOT SAY

The map spells a vattu as its consonant and a virama, which is exactly what
a dead consonant at the end of a word looks like, and it hands the
arkavattu, the vattu of ra and a dead ra the same 'ರ್'. The pdf itself keeps
all three apart - they are drawn from different glyphs - but the text does
not, so the font has to guess. A vattu that a length mark follows is a vattu
(ಶ್ರೀ), a 'C ್' behind a length mark is not a vattu of that syllable, since a
vattu is drawn before that mark, and is a dead consonant (ಎಂಪ್ಲಾಯೀಸ್, ವೆಲ್ಫೇರ್)
or the arkavattu of the syllable that follows (ನಿರ್ದೇಶನ), and a 'C ್' that a
vowel carries is a dead consonant (ಆರ್). Everything else in the middle of a
word is a vattu, kannada writing every virama between two consonants that
way, and at the end of a word everything is a vattu but the few consonants
that a kannada word is written to end with, ಸ ಶ ಥ ನ ಲ - ಎಸ್, ಲೋಕೇಶ್, ಮಂಜುನಾಥ್,
ಅಸೋಸಿಯೇಷನ್ - while a doubled consonant is a vattu all the same, so ಗಳನ್ನು and
ರಲ್ಲಿ come out whole.

A 'ರ್' is read as the vattu of ra on the consonants that ra is most often
written under, ಪ ಫ ಬ ಭ ತ ದ ಗ, and as the arkavattu everywhere else. ಕ takes
either about as often, and there the matra of the syllable decides, the ರ್ಕ
of ಸರ್ಕಾರ carrying one and the ಕ್ರ of ಕ್ರಮ not. Both readings are ordinary
kannada, so what is left of this is a real loss: ಆಶ್ರಯ comes out as ಆರ್ಶಯ and
ತರ್ಕ as ತಕ್ರ. A word that is written with a dead consonant in the middle,
which is an english loan every time, is lost the same way: ವೆಲ್ಫೇರ್ comes out
as ವ್ಲೆಫೇರ್ where the pdf leaves no space between the two halves of it.

WHAT THE EXTRACTOR LOSES BEFORE ANY OF THIS

Tunga draws a consonant and the matra i or the matra o of it in one glyph
and the maps of these pdfs have no entry at all for a number of those
ligatures. An extractor that falls back on the cid of a glyph it has no
string for, e.g. pymupdf, hands them out as the character of that cid, which
is a latin letter here because the cids are the glyph ids of Tunga itself -
ವಿ comes out as U+013B. Those cids are tokens of this font, so ವಿಷಯ comes back
whole. An extractor that drops such a glyph, e.g. pdftotext, loses them
before the converter ever sees the text, and there ವಿಷಯ is ಷಯ for good and
ನಡವಳಿಗಳು is ನಡವ ಗಳು.


AN ASCII KANNADA FONT

Nudi is the ascii kannada font family that the Karnataka gazette is published
in, and an ascii font stores no kannada at all: every glyph of it sits on a
key of the keyboard, so what comes out of the pdf is the sequence of keys the
typist pressed, read as latin1. ಸಂಪುಟ comes out as '¸ÀA¥ÀÄl' and ಕರ್ನಾಟಕ as
'PÀ£ÁðlPÀ'. The glyphs are the pieces a syllable of kannada is drawn out of
and the keys spell those pieces in the order they are drawn, which is the
order that kannada writes them in and that Tunga stores them in as well:

    [arkavattu of the syllable before] base [first half of the matra]
    vattu(s) [length mark] [signs] [arkavattu]

so fonts/kannada/nudi.py reads every syllable as a whole and writes it out
again the way unicode wants it, the arkavattu in front of the base it sits on
and the whole matra behind the vattus, exactly as fonts/kannada/tunga.py does.

WHAT THE PIECES OF A NUDI SYLLABLE ARE

A consonant that carries the vowel a is written with the glyph of the letter
and a mark that draws its head stroke, which is part of the letter in unicode
and no character of its own, so 'PÀ' is ಕ while 'Pï' - the same letter and the
virama - is ಕ್. A consonant that the vowel sign i replaces the head stroke of
is one glyph and not two, ಕಿ being 'Q'. ಫ, ಭ and ಧ are ಪ, ಬ and ದ and a stroke
on top of them that the font draws as a glyph of its own, so ಫ is '¥s' and ಫಿ
is '¦ü', the letter under the stroke being a different glyph there. ಮ is the ವ
glyph and a tail, and ಯ is the anusvara glyph, a stem and that same tail; the
tail is also the glyph that draws the vowel sign u, so a vowel sign of ಮ or ಯ
that is drawn in front of the tail sits inside the letter and ಮ is 'ªÀÄ' while
ಮೆ is 'ªÉÄ'. ವ takes a second glyph for its own vowel sign u so that ವು and ಮ
stay apart, 'ªÀÅ' against 'ªÀÄ'. Six letters - ಖ ಜ ಟ ಣ ಬ ಲ - take a second
glyph for the shape they have in front of a matra that changes it, ಖ being 'R'
at the end of a word and 'S' in ಖೆ.

WHAT AN ASCII FONT SAYS THAT A UNICODE FONT DOES NOT

The text of a Tunga pdf spells a vattu, a dead consonant and the arkavattu the
same way and fonts/kannada/tunga.py has to guess which of the three was drawn.
Nudi has a glyph of its own for every vattu, another for the arkavattu and
another for the virama, and they are different keys, so nothing here has to
guess: ಕ್ರ, ರ್ಕ and ಕ್ ರ are three different strings and ಆಶ್ರಯ and ತರ್ಕ, which
Tunga loses, come out whole.

That same split is why a virama of this font sometimes needs a zero width non
joiner behind it. A virama between two consonants binds them into a conjunct
in unicode, and in this font it does not - a conjunct is written with the
vattu glyph of its second consonant, and the virama key is the one that ends a
word or an abbreviation. So '¦J¸ï¹' is ಪಿಎಸ್‌ಸಿ and not ಪಿಎಸ್ಸಿ and '¥Á¸ï¥ÉÆÃmïð'
is ಪಾಸ್‌ಪೋರ್ಟ್ and not ಪಾಸ್ಪೋರ್ಟ್, and fonts/kannada/nudi.py puts the joiner in
to keep the two letters apart. A virama that no consonant follows needs
nothing, it already ends the word it is written in.

What is lost instead is everything the keyboard never pressed. The pdfs this
was built from draw 132 of the glyphs of the font and nothing says what the
rest of them are. The vattus are laid out in the
order of the alphabet over an unbroken run of keys and the twelve that these
documents never use are read off that run, and so are the three vowels they
never use, the thirteen vowels filling thirteen keys in that same order. The
letters are laid out in no order that holds, so a key that no document here
presses has a token only where its neighbours leave one reading open, and what
is left over is reported and dropped: ಝ, ಞ, ಢ, ಱ and ೞ have no token, nor does
the vowel sign i of ಙ, ಠ and ಢ.

The weights of Nudi differ only in what the digit keys draw. The roman ones
(Nudi01e, Nudi05e) draw the latin digits and the kannada one (Nudi01k) draws
the kannada digits, so the 15 on the cover of a gazette is ೧೫ - which is why
there are two converters, 'nudi' and 'nudi_kannada_digits', for one font.


AN 8-BIT KANNADA FONT WHOSE GLYPHS ARE NAMED TWO DIFFERENT WAYS

AkliteKndIpsita is the display font that the masthead of the Karnataka
gazette - ಕರ್ನಾಟಕ ರಾಜ್ಯಪತ್ರ, drawn as an outlined title across the top of the
front page - is set in. It is an 8-bit font like Nudi, so the text of such a
pdf is the keys the typist pressed rather than any kannada, and its glyphs
are the pieces of a syllable spelled in the order they are drawn,

    base [head stroke] [matra] vattu(s) [arkavattu]

so fonts/kannada/aklite.py reads a syllable as a whole and writes it out the
way unicode wants it, the arkavattu in front of the base it sits on and the
matra behind the vattus, exactly as fonts/kannada/nudi.py does. The head
stroke of a consonant that carries the vowel a is a glyph of its own here
too - ಕ is the ka glyph and that stroke - while ಟ, which kannada writes no
head stroke on, is the letter alone.

What is peculiar to this font is that the same glyph comes out of two of
these pdfs as two different characters. The glyphs of an 8-bit font sit on
the bytes of a latin table and carry the glyph names of whatever characters
live there, and those names say nothing about what is drawn; the byte 0xC2
draws ನ, and one producer names that glyph the macroman way (/logicalnot)
while the other names it the windows way (/Acircumflex), so an extractor
reads it back out as '¬' from the one pdf and as 'Â' from the other. Every
one of the glyphs sits on the same byte in both.

The glyph table of fonts/kannada/aklite.py is therefore keyed by the byte,
and a lexer is built over each of the two conventions. pick_lexer() chooses
between them for each run of text, by how many of the run's characters each
convention has a glyph for - a document is written by one producer and never
mixes them. U+00C2 is the only character that means anything under both, the
byte 0xE5 read the macroman way (the vattu of ya) and the byte 0xC2 read the
windows way (ನ), so a run made of nothing but that character and spaces ties,
and is read the windows way, where it is a letter that can stand on its own
rather than a vattu with no consonant in front of it.

WHAT IS NOT KNOWN ABOUT AKLITE

The pdfs this was built from draw thirteen glyphs of the font, the ones the
masthead is written with, and nothing in them says what the rest of it draws:
the subsets carry no other glyph, the encoding names no other byte, and the
ToUnicode map of the one pdf that has a map is the same list of latin glyph
names again. The thirteen were identified by rendering the outlines the
subset itself carries, one glyph at a time, and lining them up against an OCR
of the same region of the page (tesseract -l eng+kan). A byte outside them is
reported and dropped.

The masthead is drawn twice over in some of these pdfs, once for the outline
and once for the fill, and a pdf hands the overlapping pieces out as runs of
their own - so a run can begin in the middle of a syllable. A matra or a
vattu that starts one is written out where it stands rather than dropped, and
a character that is no glyph of the font at all heads no syllable, so the
arkavattu of the piece that follows a newline is not carried back across it.
